<p>
I'm not entirely sure that I've gotten the offsets nailed down yet, but they're better than the original. Once the talk airs on Dec 12, you'll get to see the results at <a href="https://emacsconf.org/2026/talks/ltx-talk/">https://emacsconf.org/2026/talks/ltx-talk/</a> .
</p>

<p>
So now I have a workflow for resynchronizing audio and video using Emacs. I really liked being able to navigate the video by using the transcript. It was easy to find the next speaker change to examine closely. I liked not having to do lots of fiddly little splits on my clips, particularly since I often found myself needing to move the offset a little earlier or later.
</p>

<p>
My next steps are probably:
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li>modify <code>compile-media.el</code> to handle <code>:av-offset</code> for both open and closed captions</li>
<li>modify subed-mpv to handle jumping to the right place</li>
<li>modify <code>subed-waveform</code> to take the offset into account when displaying the waveform</li>
</ul>

<p>
Yay editing videos by using plain text!
